course: dictionary
lesson: 12900
title: Strong words for getting rid of things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To eradicate something means to get rid of it completely, so that it no longer exists. «The town wants to eradicate the weeds from its park.» The word completely is the big clue: not some of the weeds, but every single one.
explain: Eradicate is a very strong word. You could say «remove» or «get rid of» instead, but eradicate suggests a bigger job, a plan to finish a problem for good. Try your meaning in the sentence: «The town wants to get rid of the weeds completely» still makes sense.
explain: Doctors and scientists use eradicate for illnesses. «Scientists hope to eradicate malaria» means they want to destroy the disease everywhere in the world, so that nobody catches it again.
fact: To eradicate means to get rid of something completely, so it no longer exists.
fact: «Eradicate» is a verb: eradicate, eradicates, eradicated, eradicating.
fact: Smallpox was eradicated in 1980 thanks to vaccines, so it no longer exists in the world.
fact: Scientists are still trying to eradicate diseases such as polio and malaria.
fact: Farmers and gardeners may try to eradicate pests like rats or invasive weeds, and people also talk about trying to eradicate poverty.
exercise: «The village tried to eradicate the rats from the old barn.» What does eradicate mean? | *Get rid of completely | Move to a new place | Count carefully | Feed and look after
exercise: Which phrase is closest in meaning to eradicate? | Copy and share | Find a little bit of | Look after carefully | *Destroy and remove completely
exercise: Which word completes the sentence? «Doctors hope to ___ the disease everywhere in the world.» | whisper | celebrate | decorate | *eradicate
test: «Scientists want to eradicate malaria.» What does eradicate mean in this sentence? | Ask people about it | Make it stop for a little while | Hide it in a box | *Destroy it completely everywhere
test: Which example uses eradicate for pests? | *Set traps to eradicate every rat in the warehouse | Count the rats in a picture | Put the rats out for dinner | Name the rats after your friends
exam: Which sentence uses eradicate correctly? | *The town worked hard to eradicate the weeds from its park. | Yesterday we eradicated a happy cloud. | The baby will eradicate her dinner quickly. | I eradicated my favourite song.
